From: Tom Fairfield <tom@fairfieldt.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:44:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: stream: Don't fail I/O after a CLOSE has been
 read

Since commit e120d14d03b3 ("thunderbolt: stream: Add support for busy
polling") the read and write paths check sdev->closed before they look
at the ring. Once a CLOSE packet has been read, every read returns 0 and
every write fails with -ENXIO until the device is closed, even when the
other side has opened its end again and sent more frames. Before that
commit the reader got end of file at the CLOSE, the frames behind it
could still be read, and only a blocking read or write that would have
had to wait returned 0 or -ENXIO.

A CLOSE can outlive the session that sent it. On Mac Pro 6,1 hosts
(Intel Falcon Ridge controllers connected with a Thunderbolt 2 cable),
frames sent to an end that is not open, including the CLOSE that every
close of the device sends, are delivered when that end is opened again.
Whichever end of a session closes last thus leaves a CLOSE at the other
end, and the next session there reads it and can no longer write.
Closing the device to start over sends a CLOSE back, so two hosts that
retry can keep failing each other's sessions. On a chain of six such
hosts, 9 of 25 stream sessions needed one or more retries this way and
2 never got a working stream; with the old behaviour none failed.

Check sdev->closed only where a blocking read or write would otherwise
wait, as before.

Fixes: e120d14d03b3 ("thunderbolt: stream: Add support for busy polling")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
@@ -737,7 +737,7 @@ tbstream_dev_fops_read_iter(struct kiocb *kiocb, struc
 			return ret;
 		}
 
-		if (tbstream_dev_closed(sdev) || tbstream_dev_removed(sdev)) {
+		if (tbstream_dev_removed(sdev)) {
 			mutex_unlock(&sdev->lock);
 			return 0;
 		}
@@ -749,6 +749,13 @@ tbstream_dev_fops_read_iter(struct kiocb *kiocb, struc
 
 		if (nowait)
 			return -EAGAIN;
+
+		/*
+		 * After a CLOSE, a blocking read with nothing more to read
+		 * returns end of file instead of waiting.
+		 */
+		if (tbstream_dev_closed(sdev))
+			return 0;
 
 		if (sdev->busy_poll) {
 			if (signal_pending(current))
@@ -848,7 +855,7 @@ tbstream_dev_fops_write_iter(struct kiocb *kiocb, stru
 			return ret;
 		}
 
-		if (tbstream_dev_closed(sdev) || tbstream_dev_removed(sdev)) {
+		if (tbstream_dev_removed(sdev)) {
 			mutex_unlock(&sdev->lock);
 			return -ENXIO;
 		}
@@ -861,6 +868,13 @@ tbstream_dev_fops_write_iter(struct kiocb *kiocb, stru
 		if (nowait)
 			return -EAGAIN;
 
+		/*
+		 * After a CLOSE, a blocking write that has to wait for room
+		 * in the ring fails instead.
+		 */
+		if (tbstream_dev_closed(sdev))
+			return -ENXIO;
+
 		if (sdev->busy_poll) {
 			if (signal_pending(current))
 				return -ERESTARTSYS;
